Counting is a key feature in modern logics, enabling the measurement of database changes. Researchers have demonstrated that count-based logic can be restricted to polynomial time, while the introduction of a selection operator has been crucial in achieving this. This study proposes a new logic, $P^{\mathcal{M}}_{
abla}$, designed to capture the capabilities of polynomial time without the need for a counting mechanism. $P^{\mathcal{M}}_{
abla}$ is based on a first-order logic modified by the addition of a universal-existential second-order formula $
abla X \phi(X)$, where $X$ is a binary relation and $\phi(X)$ is a first-order formula that depends on $X$. The formula $
abla X \phi(X)$ is interpreted as the existence of a subset $A$ within the universe that satisfies the first-order formula $\phi(X)$ when $X$ is replaced by the relation induced by $A$. This logic is shown to capture polynomial time in the absence of counting and extends several previous results in the field.
